From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Sat, 5 Feb 94 2:42:30 WET
Subject: [#94-2-56] Why Is There Stuff ?

  I think the best possible answer to "why is there stuff?" is 
"because there is." The question itself may be meaningless because
there might not be a reason. Consider an evolved system which may
eventually come to specialize in some function. The system, if possible,
might ask, "why do I seem to be specialized for the task?" The
answer is "because you are." There isn't any metaphysical reason.
This won't satisfy people's curiously, but consider that there may
be things in this universe which don't have a purpose of reason,
they just _are_. Like the speed of light. Why is it that value?
Because it is.

  The anthropic principle is a little stronger by answering the
"why" with "us". Why? Because we are here to observe it that way.
If it was any different, we wouldn't be here, or we would be asking
the same question.

  I do however think that we should apply reductionism until it breaks.
We should seek and continue to ask "how?" and answer it until we no longer
can. One day we may be able to model the universe back to t=0, and maybe
even further but sooner or later there will be a limit. As to the
ultimate "why?", only religion can answer that (but maybe 42 is just as
good an answer as any)

   In any case, I am less concerned as to why I'm here, or why some
cosmic hacker decided to boot up another _Game of Life_. I'm more concerned
about where I'm going, and what I think I should be doing.

-Ray

