In message <9401312318.AA26381@cleo.MasPar.Com> "Jay R. Freeman" writes:

>   I wish I knew more about methods of acting.  I have the impression that
> actors and actresses who spend lots of effort pretending to be the character
> they are playing can turn in particularly good performances.  Pretending
> 

There are two main schools of acting at the present that might illuminate
this.  They are basically utilising the methods of "pretending" and "being".
The "being" school is called the "method" school, even though the "pretending"
school uses methods as well.

The main differences between the two approaches can be illustrated in a 
simplistic way.  Take anger, for example.  If a character on stage or in
a film has to convince the audience that he is angry, how does he go about
it.  The "pretend" actor concentrates on technique.  He will observe the way
people behave when they are angry and mimic them. He will copy stances and
vocal behaviour from a wealth of experience and then try to refine this to
a point where it is instantly recognised as angry behaviour.

The method actor, on the other hand, utilises a technique called emotional
memory.  They might, for instance, think back to the last occasion in their 
lives when they were really angry.  They would then seek to recreate the 
emotion of anger within themselves and become really angry.

Both approaches have a lot to be said for them and most actors will use
whichever method helps them most at the time they are performing.  The only
draw back to the method school that I have found is knowing where to draw
the line.  I was in performance and had to have a fight with someone on
stage who took the method approach too far and nearly put me in hospital.

While it may be worth while to spend a few days living like a tramp in
order to play a tramp, how do you prepare for a part as a serial killer?
 

